hronic symptoms make this less likely", "Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (e.g., CML): elevated WBC with normal differential and constitutional symptoms", "Hyperparathyroidism: hypercalcemia without evidence of PTH elevation (not mentioned in labs)", "Hematologic Malignancy (e.g., lymphoma): hypercalcemia and constitutional symptoms, though WBC elevation is more typical for CML", "Metastatic Cancer: weight loss and constitutional symptoms, but lacks direct evidence"], "alarm_features": ["Unexplained weight loss", "Elevated leukocyte count (66,500/mm³)", "Hypercalcemia (10.9 mEq/L)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.718232044198895, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Elevated LAP is specific for myeloproliferative disorders (B1) and argues against B2. Normal differential further differentiates B1 from lymphoproliferative ca…"], "evidence_against": ["Hypercalcemia is atypical for myeloid malignancies but does not definitively exclude B1, as secondary hypercalcemia can occur in chronic processes via bone mar…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Lymphoproliferative / Plasma Cell Disorder", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.147, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Elevated LAP is specific for myeloproliferative disorders (B1) and argues against B2. Normal differential further differentiates B1 from lymphoproliferative ca…"]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.064, "danger": 0.4, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Hypercalcemia and constitutional symptoms are less common in reactive leukocytosis (B3), but B3 cannot fully explain the chronic phase or elevated LAP, maintai…"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Chronic Lymphoproliferative Neoplasm", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.04, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Other Chronic Systemic Disorders", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.031, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Polycythemia Vera", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.4788213627992633, "posterior": 0.4788213627992633, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "CML in Blast Crisis", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.1596071209330878, "posterior": 0.1596071209330878, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "phase_transformation", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Essential Thrombocythemia", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.0798035604665439, "posterior": 0.0798035604665439, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.2, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAP) score (supporting myeloproliferative neoplasm) and chronic constitutional symptoms (fatigue, weight loss) argue against leukemoid reaction (B3) …", "summary": "Elevated LAP is a strong indicator of myeloproliferative neoplasm (B1) rather than reactive processes. Chronic symptoms further reduce the likelihood of B3 (reactive leukocytosis)."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the hypercalcemia (10.9 mEq/L) with constitutional symptoms (weight loss) support a lymphoproliferative/plasma cell disorder (B2) over reactive leukocytosis (B3)?", "summary": "Hypercalcemia supports plasma cell disorders (B2) but is less specific for reactive leukocytosis (B3). However, the low LR+ for B2 in this context (0.333) weakens its probability."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the normal differential and history of a recent viral infection (cold) support reactive leukocytosis (B3) over malignant processes (B1, B2)?", "summary": "A normal differential and recent viral history suggest reactive leukocytosis (B3), but this is outweighed by the chronic presentation and elevated LAP pointing to malignancy."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of hypercalcemia (10.9 mEq/L) contradict the diagnosis of chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm (B1), given that myeloid malignancies rarely cause hypercalcemia?", "summary": "Hypercalcemia is atypical for myeloid malignancies but does not definitively exclude B1, as secondary hypercalcemia can occur in chronic processes via bone marrow involvement."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the normal leukocyte differential and elevated LAP argue against a lymphoproliferative/plasma cell disorder (B2) in favor of a myeloproliferative process (B1)?", "summary": "Elevated LAP is specific for myeloproliferative disorders (B1) and argues against B2. Normal differential further differentiates B1 from lymphoproliferative causes."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of hypercalcemia (10.9 mEq/L) and constitutional symptoms (weight loss) contradict reactive leukocytosis (B3) as the primary explanation?", "summary": "Hypercalcemia and constitutional symptoms are less common in reactive leukocytosis (B3), but B3 cannot fully explain the chronic phase or elevated LAP, maintaining moderate dissonance."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.718], ["B1.1", 0.479], ["B1.2", 0.16]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "Patient is hemodynamically stable with no acute life-threatening features. Hypercalcemia (10.9 mEq/L) is mild and not causing acute symptoms. Elevated WBC with normal differential does not indicate immediate crisis. No evidence of airway compromise, shock, or neurological deterioration.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading terminal branch (B1.1) posterior (0.479) <0.75. Dangerous alternative B1.2 (CML in Blast Crisis) remains unresolved with posterior (0.16) ≥0.10. Tree depth (level 2) < max depth (3). Requires further discriminators to differentiate between B1.1 and B1.2."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.75, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.4788213627992633, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute