s such as fever, fatigue, unintentional weight loss, or recurrent infections, as these may indicate an underlying condit\n[RAG context for 'A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue' (score=0.76):]\n  Source: Leukocytosis. > Continuing Education Activity\n  Management is directed at the underlying cause. Most patients do not require specific therapy; however, serious conditions such as leukemia, lymphoma, or hyperleukocytosis with leukostasis require urg\n[RAG context for 'In general' (score=0.59):]\n  Source: Leukocytosis. > Prognosis\n  Leukocytosis secondary to benign, nonmalignant, and treatable conditions generally carries a favorable prognosis. In contrast, leukocytosis associated with malignant lymphoproliferative disorders and \n[RAG context for 'In general' (score=0.62):]\n  Source: Leukocytosis. > Introduction\n  Leukocytosis is a common finding with a broad differential diagnosis and is typically further classified based on the predominant WBC subtype responsible for the elevation. Increases in lymphocytes, n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  recent weight loss and general fatigue → systemic manifestations of blast crisis in CML → CML in Blast Crisis\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for blast crisis-specific findings such as immature cells in peripheral blood smear, extreme leukocytosis, and organ infiltration to confirm CML progression\n  recent weight loss and general fatigue → paraneoplastic syndrome in lymphoproliferative disorders → Lymphoproliferative / Plasma Cell Disorder\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Investigate for paraneoplastic causes (e.g., elevated inflammatory markers, imaging for lymphadenopathy) to support lymphoproliferative neoplasm diagnosis\n  recent weight loss and general fatigue → myeloproliferative neoplasm-related systemic symptoms → Polycythemia Vera\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Assess for PV through complete blood count, JAK2 mutation testing, and bone marrow biopsy if splenomegaly is confirmed\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
